# Marquise Cut Diamond Ideal Proportions Guide {{year}}

## TL;DR: 5 Marquise Proportion Mistakes That Hurt Fire

**Marquise cut diamond ideal proportions** decide whether your stone catches light or looks dead. GIA does not give marquise a cut grade, so you have to read the numbers yourself. The 5 proportion mistakes buyers make most often:

- **Mistake 1 — Depth under 58%.** Light leaks through the pavilion. Face-up looks flat and dark.
- **Mistake 2 — Table over 62%.** A wide table kills fire. You lose the rainbow flash marquise is supposed to show.
- **Mistake 3 — Length-to-width ratio outside 1.75–2.10.** Narrower looks like a navette sliver. Wider stretches thin. Both silhouettes read as wrong.
- **Mistake 4 — Girdle "Thick" or "Very Thick".** Up to 7% of the carat weight hides in a thick girdle. You pay for weight you cannot see from the top.
- **Mistake 5 — Pavilion angle below 40.6° or above 41.8°.** This is the single biggest driver of the bow-tie effect — the dark line down the centre of a poorly-cut marquise.

→ Our pick: filter Blue Nile for marquise 1.75–2.00 L:W, depth 59–62%, table 54–57%, VG+ polish and symmetry → [see live marquise diamond inventory on Blue Nile](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Priority:** Pavilion angle and depth matter more than table percentage. Fix pavilion first, depth second, L:W third. Table and crown angle are secondary. If you have 60 seconds to vet a stone, check pavilion angle and look at the HD video for a bow-tie — that is 80% of the decision.

## Expert Summary

Marquise cut diamond ideal proportions sit in a tighter window than most buyers realise. The sweet spot: depth **59–62%**, table **54–57%**, L:W **1.75–2.10**, girdle **Thin to Slightly Thick**, pavilion angle **40.6–41.8°**, crown angle **32–36°**, polish and symmetry **Very Good or Excellent**. Miss any one of these numbers and you get a visible bow-tie, a flat face-up, wasted carat weight, or a silhouette that reads as off. GIA does not give marquise a cut grade — unlike round brilliant — so the responsibility to verify sits with the buyer. This guide walks each number, why it matters, and which filter settings surface the correctly-cut stones on Blue Nile's current marquise inventory. Short version: use the [live Blue Nile marquise search](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with the filter set listed below, then verify each shortlisted stone on HD video.

## Why GIA Does Not Give Marquise a Cut Grade (and What That Means for You)

GIA assigns a cut grade — Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, Poor — to **round brilliant diamonds only**. Every other shape, including marquise, oval, pear, cushion, and emerald, receives polish and symmetry grades but no overall cut rating.

The reason is physics. Round brilliant has one geometry that maximises light return, so GIA built a grading model around it. Marquise can be cut in dozens of valid proportion combinations, each tuned for a different look — elongated silhouette, bolder face-up, finger-covering spread. GIA refused to collapse that variety into a single grade.

The practical consequence is that **the marquise on Blue Nile labelled "Ideal" or "Super Ideal" is a retailer designation, not a GIA grade**. You cannot trust the label alone. You have to read the actual numbers on the certificate. Our [round diamond ideal proportions guide](/round-diamond-ideal-proportions) covers the GIA Excellent grade system for round brilliants in detail — marquise does not get that benefit.

For marquise, here is the shortcut: the stone performs well if (a) depth is 59–62%, (b) table is 54–57%, (c) L:W is 1.75–2.10, (d) girdle is Thin to Slightly Thick, (e) pavilion angle is 40.6–41.8°, and (f) polish and symmetry are both Very Good or Excellent. Nothing else matters at this level of detail.

If a stone misses even one of these, you can still buy it — but you should know what you are trading. The next sections walk each proportion mistake and the exact cost.

## Mistake #1 — Depth Under 58% Lets Light Escape

Depth percentage is the ratio of the diamond's height to its width at the widest point. For marquise cut diamond ideal proportions, depth should sit between **59% and 62.3%**.

Below 58%, the stone is too shallow. Light enters the crown, hits the pavilion at a shallow angle, and exits straight through the bottom instead of bouncing back up to your eye. The face-up appearance becomes flat, washed-out, and glassy. In jewellery-industry language this is called "windowing" — you can almost see through the stone from the top.

The symptom is easy to spot once you know what to look for. Hold the stone over a printed line or black-and-white text. If you can see the line through the diamond, depth is too shallow. A correctly cut marquise should bounce light back at you and reveal nothing of what is behind it.

Depth above 64% is the opposite problem. The stone becomes too deep. Light enters, bounces around inside the pavilion, and eventually leaks out the sides — but by that point you have paid for carat weight that is hidden below the girdle. You get a smaller face-up size for the same weight. This connects to the girdle issue we cover in Mistake #4.

The 59–62% window is the balance point. Light enters the crown, bounces off the pavilion at the correct angle, and returns to your eye — this is what fire and brilliance look like in practice. On Blue Nile you can filter marquise diamonds by depth; the current [marquise inventory with depth 59-62%](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&CaratTo=1.50&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the correct starting shortlist.

**What this costs if you ignore it:** A 1-carat marquise with 56% depth looks like a 0.75-carat marquise with 60% depth from the top. You paid for 1 carat and visually got 0.75. That is roughly $800–$1,200 of value lost at retail prices.

## Mistake #2 — Table Over 62% Kills Fire

Table percentage is the width of the top (flat) facet of the diamond divided by the total width. For marquise cut diamond ideal proportions, the table should sit between **54% and 57%**.

The table controls how much light enters the stone and how it fractures into fire. A narrow table (below 53%) means less light gets in — the stone can look dim. A wide table (above 62%) means too much light enters without being bent by the crown facets, so it comes back out as white light rather than rainbow dispersion.

Marquise diamonds are brilliant-cut — they are designed to produce fire. If you buy a marquise with a 65% table, you are paying for a brilliant-cut aesthetic and getting step-cut behaviour. The stone will look large and bright but flat. The rainbow flash will be absent.

Our [round diamond table percentage guide](/round-diamond-table-percentage) covers the exact same principle for round brilliants, where the sweet spot is also 54–57%. For marquise the physics is identical — the shape just makes the effect more pronounced because the elongated outline already compromises some brilliance.

**What this costs:** The visual difference between a 56% table marquise and a 65% table marquise is extreme. Side by side, the 56% stone looks alive and sparkly. The 65% stone looks like glass. Buyers who buy wide-table marquises almost always return them within 30 days when they see them in natural light.

## Mistake #3 — Length-to-Width Ratio Outside 1.75–2.10

Length-to-width ratio (L:W) measures how elongated the marquise is. A pure navette shape would be about 2.5 — very long and narrow. A near-oval marquise would be 1.5. The accepted range for marquise cut diamond ideal proportions is **1.75–2.10**.

Why this range? Below 1.75, the stone starts to look like an oval with pointed ends. The silhouette loses the elongation that makes marquise visually distinctive. Above 2.10, the stone becomes thin and sliver-like — the "navette" look that goes in and out of fashion but is harder to set and more fragile at the tips.

The sweet spot for most buyers is **1.90–2.00**. This gives a classic marquise outline — elongated but not fragile, bold but not stretched. A 1-carat marquise at 1.95 L:W is about 11mm long and 5.7mm wide. The same carat weight at 1.75 L:W is 10mm long and 5.7mm wide (chunkier), and at 2.10 L:W is 11.8mm long and 5.6mm wide (longer, thinner).

L:W is a personal preference as much as a proportion rule. If you have narrow fingers, a 2.00+ L:W elongates them. If you have wider fingers, a 1.80 L:W fills more of the finger and looks more proportional. There is no wrong answer within 1.75–2.10 — just an answer that fits.

Blue Nile's marquise filter lets you specify L:W ratio as part of the search. The current [marquise diamond inventory filtered to 1.75–2.10 L:W](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the correct starting pool.

**What this costs:** A marquise outside the 1.75–2.10 range sells at a discount of 10–15% compared to one inside the range. The reason is resale market demand — buyers specifically search for the 1.90–2.00 window, so stones outside that window trade at a liquidity discount.

## Mistake #4 — Girdle "Thick" or "Very Thick" Hides Weight

The girdle is the thin outer edge of the diamond — the widest point around the perimeter. GIA grades girdle thickness on a scale: Extremely Thin, Very Thin, Thin, Medium, Slightly Thick, Thick, Very Thick, Extremely Thick.

For marquise cut diamond ideal proportions, you want **Thin to Slightly Thick**. Extremely Thin is risky — the girdle can chip, especially at the pointed tips where marquise diamonds are most vulnerable. Thick or Very Thick is wasteful — up to 7% of the diamond's weight sits in the girdle, which you cannot see from the top of the stone.

This is a hidden cost that most buyers never check. A 1.5-carat marquise with a Very Thick girdle has roughly the same face-up appearance as a 1.4-carat marquise with a Medium girdle. You paid for 0.1 carat of weight that is invisible from the top. At $5,000-per-carat prices that is roughly $500 wasted.

Our [round diamond girdle thickness guide](/round-diamond-girdle-thickness) covers the same principle in detail. The economic logic holds for every diamond shape: thick girdle = hidden weight = less visible stone per dollar.

The exception is marquise with pointed tips — Thin girdle at the tip zone is more fragile than Medium. If you buy a marquise with Thin girdle, the setting must have V-tip prongs that fully cover both points. Without V-prong protection, a Thin-girdle marquise is a chip waiting to happen.

Blue Nile displays girdle thickness in the stone details page. Filter for Thin to Slightly Thick and verify the specific girdle grade before buying.

## Mistake #5 — Pavilion Angle Outside 40.6–41.8° Causes Bow-Tie

Pavilion angle is the angle between the pavilion facets and the girdle plane. For round brilliants it is tightly controlled — GIA Excellent requires 40.6–41.8°, and our [round diamond pavilion angle guide](/round-diamond-pavilion-angle) walks through the exact mechanics.

For marquise, the same angle range matters but the consequence of missing it is more visible. If the pavilion angle is wrong, light that should bounce back to your eye instead bounces across the stone from one side to the other, creating a dark area in the middle. In marquise this dark area forms a classic bow-tie shape — the horizontal line that cuts across the centre of the stone.

The bow-tie effect is almost impossible to see in a static photo. You need the HD video that Blue Nile provides on every stone — rotate the diamond and watch for the dark line that sweeps across the middle as the stone turns. If you see a strong bow-tie, the pavilion is wrong.

Light bow-tie is unavoidable — about 40–50% of all marquise diamonds show some bow-tie, even those with perfect pavilion angles. Our existing analysis on [the elongation trade-off in oval vs marquise diamonds](/oval-vs-marquise-diamond) covers the comparative bow-tie risk. The question is not "is there a bow-tie" but "is the bow-tie too dark to accept." Strong bow-tie = walk away. Faint bow-tie that disappears when you move the stone = acceptable.

The crown angle (**32–36°** sweet spot) matters too but has less bow-tie impact. Pavilion angle is 80% of the bow-tie decision. See our [round diamond crown angle guide](/round-diamond-crown-angle) for the full crown-side physics.

Blue Nile does not expose pavilion angle in the default filter view — you have to open each stone's detail page and read the certificate. This is tedious but non-negotiable if you want to avoid bow-tie.

**What this costs:** A marquise with wrong pavilion angle sells for 15–25% less at resale. It is also the most common reason buyers return a marquise within the 30-day window.

## The Fire Floor — The Pavilion-Angle Threshold Below Which Marquise Dies

Here is a concept we call **The Fire Floor**. It is the pavilion angle below which marquise diamonds lose dispersion so dramatically that even a trained eye sees the difference across a room.

The Fire Floor sits at **40.4°**. Below this threshold, light hits the pavilion facets at an angle too shallow to bend into its component colours (which is what dispersion — fire — actually is). The stone still returns light, but as flat white light rather than the rainbow flash that defines a brilliant-cut diamond.

Above 40.4°, dispersion comes back online. By 40.6° (the GIA Excellent lower bound for round brilliants), fire is strong. By 41.0° fire peaks. By 41.8° fire is still good but you start to see internal reflection — the stone can look "sleepy" if the angle climbs further.

The Fire Floor is important because it is a hard boundary, not a gradient. A marquise at 40.3° does not have 90% of the fire of a marquise at 40.5°. It has roughly 30%. The dispersion collapse is sudden, not linear. This is why we treat 40.6° as the minimum acceptable pavilion angle, not a target — it is the floor you refuse to go below.

Practical application: when reviewing a GIA certificate, if the pavilion angle reads 40.3° or lower, the stone is below The Fire Floor regardless of its other proportions. Walk away. There are enough correctly-cut marquises in the market that you do not need to compromise on this.

## The Scaling Window — Why L:W + Table Must Be Paired

The second named concept is **The Scaling Window** — the combination of L:W ratio and table percentage that keeps marquise diamonds visually balanced across different carat weights.

The problem it solves: a 1-carat marquise and a 3-carat marquise cannot use identical proportions and look proportional. At 1 carat, a wide table (56–57%) and L:W 1.90 gives a bright, broad stone. Scale that proportion to 3 carats and the same table percentage reads as an enormous flat top — the stone looks heavy and chunky rather than elegant.

Here is The Scaling Window rule: **as carat weight increases, L:W should increase slightly and table percentage should decrease slightly** to maintain visual balance.

| Carat tier | Target L:W | Target table | Target depth | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0.50 – 0.75 ct | 1.80 – 1.90 | 56 – 58% | 59 – 61% | Chunkier shape at small sizes reads bolder on the finger |
| 1.00 – 1.50 ct | 1.90 – 2.00 | 55 – 57% | 59 – 61% | Classic marquise sweet spot; strongest resale market |
| 2.00 – 2.50 ct | 1.95 – 2.05 | 54 – 56% | 60 – 62% | Narrower table preserves fire as the stone scales up |
| 3.00 ct+ | 2.00 – 2.10 | 54 – 55% | 60 – 62% | Elongation reads refined rather than heavy at statement sizes |

**What this means:** at smaller carat weights, a chunkier marquise looks better. At larger carat weights, a more elongated marquise reads as refined rather than heavy. Table percentage should narrow at larger sizes to preserve fire — a 3-carat stone with a 58% table loses dispersion that a 1-carat stone with the same table retains, because the proportional distance between facets changes.

**Verdict:** Use this table as the starting filter when shopping. If you are buying a 1-carat marquise, filter Blue Nile for L:W 1.85–2.00 and table 55–57%. If you are buying a 2-carat marquise, filter for L:W 1.95–2.05 and table 54–56%. Scaling the proportions to the carat weight is the single biggest move you can make for face-up appearance.

## How to Read a GIA Report for Marquise Proportions

GIA certificates for marquise diamonds contain all the data you need in two places: the Proportions diagram on the right side of the report, and the Grade column on the left. Here is what to read, in order.

**1. Measurements (top of report).** Format: `length × width × depth` in millimetres. Calculate L:W by dividing length by width. For a stone listed as 10.52 × 5.58 × 3.44, L:W = 10.52 / 5.58 = 1.885. That falls inside the 1.75–2.10 sweet spot.

**2. Depth percentage.** Listed below the measurements. For the same stone, depth = 3.44 / 5.58 = 61.6%. That is inside the 59–62% window.

**3. Table percentage.** Also listed below measurements. Target 54–57%.

**4. Crown angle.** On the Proportions diagram, labelled on the upper half. Target 32–36°.

**5. Pavilion angle.** On the Proportions diagram, labelled on the lower half. Target 40.6–41.8°. This is the number that drives bow-tie — read it carefully.

**6. Girdle.** Listed as text, e.g., "Thin to Slightly Thick, Faceted." Target Thin to Slightly Thick. Avoid Very Thin or Thick+.

**7. Culet.** Should read "None" or "Pointed." Any culet facet larger than Small is visible from the top and reduces face-up aesthetic.

**8. Polish and Symmetry.** On the left side under Grading Results. Target Very Good or Excellent on both. Good is acceptable only on heavily discounted stones; Fair is a walk-away.

For a more detailed walk-through of GIA certificate reading applied to round diamonds (where the mechanics are identical), see our [round diamond ideal proportions guide](/round-diamond-ideal-proportions). The structure of the certificate is the same for marquise — only the acceptable ranges change.

