## Lab vs Natural: Does Proportion Matter More for Lab Marquises?

Short answer: yes, slightly. Here is why.

Lab-grown marquises come from the same cutting houses as natural diamonds. The cutters use the same wheels, the same angles, and the same quality control standards. In theory, lab and natural marquises of the same cut grade perform identically.

In practice, lab marquises are cut for yield more often than natural marquises. The lab-grown rough is cheaper per carat than natural rough, so cutters are more willing to maximise finished carat weight at the cost of ideal proportions. This shows up as slightly deeper average depths (62–64% is more common in lab than natural), slightly thicker average girdles, and slightly higher pavilion angle variance.

The practical implication: when buying lab marquise, apply the same proportion filters you would use for natural. Do not accept depth 63% in lab just because the price is lower — the stone will still look worse than a lab marquise at depth 60%. Our [existing marquise cut diamond guide](/marquise-cut-diamond) covers the lab-vs-natural pricing framework in detail.

The upside for lab buyers: because lab inventory turns over faster and prices are lower, you can afford to be pickier on proportions. A 1-carat natural marquise meeting all 5 proportion criteria costs around $2,500 at Blue Nile. The lab equivalent — same filters — costs $900. For $900, you have no excuse to settle on proportions. Filter aggressively and only buy correctly cut stones.

Browse: [1-carat lab-grown marquise diamonds with VG+ polish and symmetry](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&CaratTo=1.24&IsLab=true&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

> **Farzana's Verdict:** The myth that lab diamonds are "always worse cut" than natural is outdated. Modern lab marquises from the main cutting houses (Blue Nile's main lab suppliers in India and Israel) are cut to the same standards as natural. The variance I see is small — about 5% of lab marquises are cut for yield in a way that natural would never be. The other 95% match or exceed natural proportions. Filter for the same depth, L:W, and pavilion angle in lab as you would in natural. If you do that, you get the same visual performance at 40-60% of the price.

## Marquise vs Other Fancy Shapes: Proportion Comparison

| Shape | Ideal depth | Ideal table | Ideal L:W | Pavilion angle | Bow-tie risk | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Marquise | 59 – 62% | 54 – 57% | 1.75 – 2.10 | 40.6 – 41.8° | High (40-50%) | Hardest fancy shape to buy without video verification |
| Oval | 58 – 62% | 53 – 63% | 1.35 – 1.50 | 40.6 – 41.8° | High (50%+) | More forgiving L:W range but same bow-tie risk |
| Pear | 58 – 62% | 53 – 63% | 1.45 – 1.75 | 40.6 – 41.8° | Medium (30%) | Pointed tip creates vulnerability but less bow-tie |
| Cushion | 61 – 68% | 61 – 68% | 1.00 – 1.10 | 59 – 65° (modified) | Low | Different pavilion geometry — bow-tie rare |
| Emerald | 61 – 67% | 60 – 68% | 1.30 – 1.50 | Step cut | None | Step cut does not produce bow-tie — different physics |
| Radiant | 61 – 67% | 60 – 68% | 1.00 – 1.50 | Modified brilliant | Low | Brilliant pavilion in rectangular outline avoids bow-tie |

**Verdict:** Marquise is the fancy shape most sensitive to proportion errors because the elongated outline amplifies any bow-tie or depth problem. If you are new to fancy shapes, cushion or radiant are more forgiving starting points. If you specifically want marquise, commit to video verification on every stone — the risk of a bad-looking marquise is too high to buy on specs alone.

For a shape-by-shape visual comparison across all cuts, see our [diamond shapes guide](/diamond-shapes-guide) and the [diamond cut guide](/diamond-cut-guide) that covers brilliant vs step cut mechanics in depth.

## Marquise Cut Diamond Setting Rules Driven by Proportions

Proportions dictate setting choice. If your marquise has a Thin girdle at the tips, you need V-tip prongs — full coverage at both points — or the tips will chip within a year of daily wear. A marquise with Medium or thicker girdle can tolerate four-prong or six-prong settings as long as two of those prongs cover the tips.

Bezel settings fully protect the tips and are the safest option for daily-wear marquise rings. The trade-off is that bezels reduce light return by 5-8% because they cover a portion of the pavilion. If you want maximum sparkle, pick a prong-and-tip-protector setting. If you want maximum durability, pick a bezel.

Halos work well on marquise because the halo stones draw the eye to the centre and distract from any faint bow-tie. A pavé halo can mask a mild bow-tie that would be more visible in a solitaire setting. If your marquise has a minor bow-tie and you still want to buy it, choose a halo setting — the perceived flaw disappears.

