| [Marquise 3-Stone](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/marquise-cut-diamond-three-stone-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-315107?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | 14K Yellow Gold | $1,970 | Three-stone complexity hides any faint Severity 2 bow-tie | Lowest-price mounting with full bow-tie insurance |
| [Floral Marquise](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/floral-marquise-diamond-engagement-ring-in-18k-white-gold-item-203738?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | 18K White Gold | $2,210 | Floral halo adds visual depth; 18K metal lasts longer than 14K | Best vintage-leaning mounting under $2,500 |
| [Pavé Silhouette 3-Stone](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/pave-silhouette-diamond-three-stone-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-310849?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | 14K Yellow Gold | $2,580 | Pavé band + 3 stones = maximum visual complexity | Best choice if your center stone is Severity 3 |
| [Petite Cathedral Pavé](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/petite-cathedral-pave-diamond-engagement-ring-in-platinum-1-6-ct-tw-item-194341?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Platinum | $2,010 | Cathedral arch raises center stone 2-4mm; shows off good proportions | Best for Severity 1 marquise you want to display |

**Verdict:** If your marquise meets all 5 proportion rules (Severity 1-2 bow-tie), the solitaire or cathedral mounting shows it off best. If it misses one rule (Severity 3 bow-tie), the three-stone or floral halo mounting masks the flaw. The $1,970-$2,580 range covers every bow-tie masking strategy for a 1-carat center stone. Total ring cost = mounting + center stone, so a complete 1-carat GIA marquise ring from Blue Nile lands in the $4,000-$5,500 range at this quality tier.

## Marquise Cut Diamond Buying Checklist (Print-Ready)

Before clicking "buy" on any marquise, verify every item:

1. ✅ Depth is between 59% and 62% (never below 58%)
2. ✅ Table is between 54% and 57% (never above 62%)
3. ✅ L:W ratio is between 1.75 and 2.10
4. ✅ Pavilion angle is between 40.6° and 41.8° (verify on certificate)
5. ✅ Crown angle is between 32° and 36°
6. ✅ Girdle is Thin, Medium, or Slightly Thick (never Thick+)
7. ✅ Culet is None or Pointed
8. ✅ Polish is Very Good or Excellent
9. ✅ Symmetry is Very Good or Excellent
10. ✅ GIA or IGI (for lab) certified — no uncertified stones
11. ✅ HD video reviewed — no visible bow-tie dark line in rotation
12. ✅ Setting has V-tip prongs or bezel at the points

If a stone fails any one of these, filter it out and keep searching. Blue Nile's live marquise inventory is big enough that you do not have to settle. Our [round diamond buying checklist pattern applied to marquise](/round-diamond-ideal-proportions) uses the same discipline — this is how experts actually buy.

> **Farzana's Verdict:** Most of the regret I see on marquise purchases comes from skipping steps 4 and 11 on this checklist. Buyers filter on carat, color, and clarity, pick the cheapest stone that matches, and skip the pavilion angle check and the video verification. Then the stone arrives and they see the bow-tie in person for the first time. By then it is often outside the return window. Do the pavilion check. Watch the video. Twice. That is the entire difference between a marquise you love and one you regret.

## Final Verdict: Best Marquise Cut Diamond Proportions by Buyer Scenario

Four buyer profiles cover 90% of marquise shoppers. Here is which proportion set to filter for in each case.

**Best for the first-time marquise buyer (budget $2K–$4K)**
Filter for 1.00–1.25 ct, L:W 1.90–2.00, depth 59–61%, table 55–57%, pavilion angle 40.6–41.4°, girdle Thin to Slightly Thick, VG+ polish and symmetry. This is the deepest inventory tier on Blue Nile and the easiest tier to find a stone that passes all 5 proportion checks. → [See the full 1-carat marquise shortlist with these filters](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&CaratTo=1.24&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the lab-grown value buyer (budget $900–$2,500)**
Filter for 1.00–2.00 ct lab IGI, L:W 1.90–2.00, depth 60–62%, table 55–57%, pavilion angle 40.6–41.8°. Lab inventory lets you go bigger for the same money, so push to 1.5–2.0 ct rather than compromising on proportions. → [See lab marquise diamonds in the 1-2 carat tier](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&CaratTo=1.99&IsLab=true&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the statement-size buyer (budget $7,500+)**
Filter for 2.00 ct+ natural GIA, L:W 1.95–2.05, depth 60–62%, table 54–56%, pavilion angle 40.8–41.6°. At this size, pavilion angle matters even more — bow-tie is more visible on larger stones. Narrow the pavilion filter to the tighter 40.8–41.6° window and reject anything outside it. → [See 2 carat+ natural marquise diamonds](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=2.00&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the resale-conscious buyer**
Filter for 1.50 ct natural GIA at the 1.5-carat "magic size" — resale demand is strongest here. L:W 1.90–2.00, depth 60–62%, table 54–56%, pavilion angle 40.8–41.4° (tightest acceptable window), VG+ polish and symmetry, F or G color, VS2 or better clarity. The tighter the proportion window, the better the stone trades on the resale market. → [See 1.5 carat marquise diamonds with GIA certification](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.50&CaratTo=1.59&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

## Frequently Asked Questions

### What are the ideal proportions for a marquise cut diamond?

Marquise cut diamond ideal proportions are: depth 59–62%, table 54–57%, length-to-width ratio 1.75–2.10, pavilion angle 40.6–41.8°, crown angle 32–36°, girdle Thin to Slightly Thick, polish and symmetry Very Good or Excellent. GIA does not assign an overall cut grade to marquise, so the buyer has to verify these numbers from the certificate directly.

### What is the best length-to-width ratio for a marquise diamond?

The best length-to-width ratio for marquise is 1.90–2.00 for most buyers. This gives the classic elongated marquise silhouette without stretching too thin. Ratios below 1.75 look like ovals with pointed ends; ratios above 2.10 look fragile and sliver-like. If you have narrow fingers, trend toward 2.00+. If you have wider fingers, 1.80–1.90 fills the finger better.

### How do I avoid the bow-tie effect on a marquise diamond?

Verify the pavilion angle is between 40.6° and 41.8° on the GIA certificate and watch the HD video of the stone rotating. If the dark line down the centre moves across the stone as it turns and remains strong, the bow-tie is too severe — walk away. Faint bow-tie that disappears when the stone moves is acceptable. About 40–50% of marquise diamonds show some bow-tie, so a complete absence is rare.

### Does GIA give marquise diamonds a cut grade?

No. GIA assigns an overall cut grade only to round brilliant diamonds. Marquise, oval, pear, cushion, emerald, and other fancy shapes receive polish and symmetry grades but no overall cut rating. This means marquise labelled "Ideal" or "Super Ideal" on retailer sites is a retailer designation, not a GIA grade. Verify proportions on the certificate yourself.

### What depth is too shallow for a marquise diamond?

Depth below 58% is too shallow. Light leaks through the pavilion instead of bouncing back to your eye. The stone looks flat, washed-out, and glassy. You can test this at home — hold the stone over a printed line; if you can see the line through the stone, depth is too shallow.

### What girdle thickness is acceptable for a marquise diamond?

Thin, Medium, or Slightly Thick are all acceptable. Avoid Very Thin (chip risk at the pointed tips) and Thick, Very Thick, or Extremely Thick (hidden carat weight — you pay for stone you cannot see from the top).

### Is pavilion angle more important than table percentage for marquise?

Yes. Pavilion angle is the single biggest driver of the bow-tie effect, which is the most common marquise cut flaw. Table percentage affects fire but has less dramatic impact. If you have to prioritise one number, pavilion angle wins — a stone with correct pavilion and slightly wide table still performs well; a stone with correct table and wrong pavilion shows a visible bow-tie that no other proportion can fix.

### How much more does a correctly cut marquise cost than one with poor proportions?

A marquise meeting all 5 proportion criteria costs 10–15% more at retail than a comparable stone with one or two proportion issues. The premium is small compared to the visual improvement — correctly cut marquises hold resale value 15–25% better than poorly cut stones because the resale market specifically searches for the correct proportions.

### Can lab-grown marquise diamonds have the same proportions as natural?

Yes. Lab-grown marquise diamonds from major cutting houses are cut to the same proportion standards as natural. About 5% of lab marquises are cut for yield (slightly deeper, slightly wider girdle) because lab rough is cheaper, so cutters are more willing to maximise finished weight. Apply the same proportion filters when shopping lab as you would for natural.

### What is the best pavilion angle for a marquise diamond?

40.6° to 41.8° is the ideal range. The sweet spot inside that range is 41.0°, where fire peaks. Below 40.4° — what we call The Fire Floor — dispersion collapses dramatically and the stone looks dull even if other proportions are correct. Above 42°, light bounces too many times inside the pavilion before exiting, and the stone can look "sleepy" or lifeless.

### Should I buy a marquise with a culet?

No. Culet should be None or Pointed. Any culet facet larger than Small is visible from the top of the stone as a small dark circle or hexagon, which disrupts the marquise's clean elongated appearance. GIA grades culets from None/Pointed down through Very Small, Small, Medium, and larger. Reject anything larger than Small.

### Do all marquise diamonds show a bow-tie?

About 40–50% show some degree of bow-tie, even those with correct pavilion angles. The bow-tie is caused by light exiting through the sides of the pavilion rather than bouncing back to your eye — it is partially a geometric consequence of the elongated outline. The question is severity: faint, moving bow-tie is acceptable; strong, static bow-tie is not.

### What setting protects marquise diamond tips best?

V-tip prongs — prongs shaped like a V that fully cover both pointed ends of the marquise — are the best protection. Bezel settings also fully protect the tips but reduce light return by 5–8%. Four-prong and six-prong settings work only if two of the prongs are positioned to cover the tips; otherwise the exposed points chip within a year of daily wear.

### How do I tell if a marquise diamond has correct proportions from an HD video?

Rotate the stone slowly in the video. Watch for three things: (1) does the centre of the stone go dark as it rotates — if yes, bow-tie is too strong; (2) does the stone sparkle evenly across the surface or only near the edges — even sparkle is good; (3) do you see a clear "window" effect where you can see through to the back of the setting — if yes, depth is too shallow. Any one of these failures means the proportions are wrong.

